Your job is to find what is WRONG or DANGEROUS in the proposed design, not to praise it.

=== THE PROPOSED DESIGN (owner-set direction, docs/ARCHITECTURE_home_and_workers_2026-08-28.md) ===
ONE HOME: a small Linux VM on Google Cloud (separate GCP project from the owner's medical
records project "Arch"; no public IP; Tailscale-only). The home holds: the RECORD (sqlite
chief.db), the GATEKEEPER (the only writer of merge/deploy/spend), the web APP the owner's
phone opens, and the VOICE token endpoint + streaming.
MANY WORKERS: the owner's MacBook (local ollama model, small jobs), a Mac Studio arriving
~late Sept 2026 (heavy local models + claude-cli/codex builders), and a cloud worker on the
home box as a backup. Workers connect over the tailnet, CLAIM jobs they are suited for, run
the builder and the reviewer panel locally, and report events/artifacts/head_version/result
back THROUGH a home HTTP API — never by touching the sqlite file.
A worker that is off is off: unclaimed jobs wait or go to another worker; in-flight jobs are
marked STALLED on heartbeat loss and picked up when it returns.
The wall (separate OS user for agents, keys never reach builders — docs/THE_WALL_owner_steps.md,
queue task 13) is to be built "on the home, once", for Linux.
Arch (the medical repo) stays off-limits to every worker.

=== WHAT I FOUND IN THE CODE (verify each of these; correct me where I'm wrong) ===
1. THERE IS NO PULL LOOP TODAY. db/jobs.py::claim_next_job exists and is atomic, but grep
   shows it is called ONLY from harness/tests/test_jobs.py and harness/scripts/smoke_dispatch.py.
   The live path is PUSH, IN-PROCESS: dispatch.py::dispatch_local (line ~181-184) sets status
   'in_progress' and then calls executor.start_in_background(job_id) — a thread in the same
   process as the record. So the architecture doc's claim "workers ask the record for work;
   moving workers to other machines is plumbing on a design that already assumes it" looks
   false to me. Also claim_next_job filters on status='todo', which dispatch_local never
   leaves a job in.
2. claim_next_job claims BY builder_seat, has NO worker identity, NO lease, NO expiry, NO
   heartbeat. jobs has no worker column. 'stalled' is not in the jobs.status CHECK constraint
   (schema.sql ~279). attempts increments per claim with no ceiling.
3. THE PANEL AND ALL RECORDING ARE IN-PROCESS TODAY. executor.run_job calls
   gauntlet.run_panel; each reviewer thread opens its OWN sqlite connection (gauntlet.py
   _review_one) and calls db.jobs.record_verdict / record_artifact / set_status directly.
   The schema triggers verify a verdict's tier/family against the seats table but CANNOT
   verify that a review actually happened. artifacts.captured_by ('harness'|'playwright'|
   'model') is enforced by a trigger that only reads the value in the row — trust today comes
   from the writer being in-process, not from anything checkable.
4. SPEND: gauntlet._reserve_review_budget imports gatekeeper and calls gatekeeper.spend
   in-process on the panel's own writable connection. Caps are DB triggers on INSERT INTO
   usage (guard_daily_cap_is_hard / build / review / monthly).
5. GATEKEEPER: gatekeeper.serve() is LOOPBACK-ONLY by explicit design ("the tailnet is other
   devices, and the whole point of this process is that reaching it is harder than reaching
   the agents") + a shared token in ~/.chief/gatekeeper.token. gatekeeper.merge does REAL GIT
   WORK in a repo on the machine it runs on: _branch_tip, _diff_bundle, _commit_holds_the_
   reviewed_work, _repo_is_ready, _git_merge, and it compares the branch tip against
   jobs.head_version and the committed diff against jobs.result byte-for-byte.
6. Nothing ever pushes job/<id> anywhere (queue task 12 says so). So if the builder runs on a
   worker, the home has no git objects to verify or merge.
7. DISPATCH STAMPS THE PANEL SIZE FROM LOCAL PROBING: dispatch.panel_roster ->
   gauntlet.has_runner probes THIS MACHINE (is `claude` logged in, is `codex` logged in, is
   XAI_API_KEY set) and writes required_reviews / required_review_families onto the job
   (un-lowerable by trigger). On the home, none of those CLIs may exist.
8. VOICE: server.py::voice_token mints an OpenAI ephemeral client secret from OPENAI_API_KEY
   loaded from ~/.chief/env by _load_chief_env; the browser then talks DIRECTLY to the vendor.
   The mouth's only tool posts to /api/voice/ask (+ /api/voice/ask/stream, SSE) on the server.
   server.py binds 127.0.0.1:8787 and is exposed by `tailscale serve`. There is no auth on
   these endpoints other than being on the tailnet. A single held chief_live session
   (_chief_session) is process-global.
9. seats.toml is one file; seats are synced into the DB at server start (dispatch.sync_seats)
   from whatever machine runs the server. Metered seat = grok/xai with daily/build/review caps.

=== THE QUESTIONS I NEED YOU TO ATTACK ===
(1) WORKER PROTOCOL. Design/critique: claim (what is the unit of matching — seat? capability
    labels? project?), lease + heartbeat + renewal, what STALLED means and who sets it,
    resume-vs-rerun (the dead worker's clone is on the dead machine), idempotent reporting
    (events, artifacts, verdicts, head_version, result) — exactly-once or at-least-once with
    dedupe keys? How does the frozen bundle / clone source reach a worker: git over the
    tailnet from a bare repo on the home, or the home serving bundles? How does a worker PROVE
    it ran the reviewed bytes? WHAT CAN A COMPROMISED OR BUGGY WORKER DO TO THE RECORD — be
    concrete and rank it. Is "the worker runs the panel and reports verdicts" salvageable at
    all, or does it destroy the gauntlet's meaning?
(2) WHICH SCHEMA GUARDS BREAK when the writer is an HTTP API rather than in-process Python?
    Go guard by guard through harness/db/schema.sql. Which become self-declared claims over
    the wire? What must move behind the gatekeeper, and what new guards/columns are required?
(3) VOICE/APP ON THE HOME. What changes in the token step and the SSE streaming when the home
    is a cloud VM in a GCP region rather than the laptop on the owner's desk? Latency budget
    for the mouth->ask_chief round trip (Tailscale direct vs DERP relay), auth on the API when
    it is no longer "my own laptop", the process-global held session, and failure modes when
    the tailnet or the VM is down. ~90% of use is voice; the owner drives while using it.
(4) SEATS PER WORKER. Local model only on laptop/Studio; API/metered seats where the keys are.
    How do budget caps stay ONE record when spend can be initiated on three machines? Can a
    worker holding a provider key be prevented from spending without a reservation? Should
    metered seats be forbidden on remote workers entirely?
(5) THE CLOUD HOME'S HARDENING on Linux: OS users, systemd, zero inbound, Tailscale
    (serve vs funnel), where keys live, backup/restore of the sqlite record (WAL), and whether
    "the wall built on the home once" is even the right place — my view is the wall protects
    against BUILDER agents, and builders run on the macOS workers, not the home. Am I right?
(6) MIGRATION ORDER so nothing is migrated twice (the Studio arrives AFTER the home exists).
    What is the smallest first step that is reversible? What must NOT be built yet?

=== RULES OF THIS PROJECT (binding) ===
See /AGENTS.md. Highlights: text-first (voice is a skin, must never be load-bearing);
version-pin everything; Tailscale-only, nothing public; hardware-agnostic (no machine-specific
paths); no Google/Gemini AI (Google CLOUD is allowed); local output never ships without a
higher-tier cross-family review (DB trigger); providers referenced by SEAT never by name;
every spec carries a Feature Acceptance Checklist (what control exists, what it does, where
it is reachable). The owner CANNOT read code — anything he reads must be plain English.

=== OUTPUT I WANT ===
- A verdict: GO / ADJUST / STOP on the design as proposed, with your reasoning.
- The ranked failure modes, most dangerous first, each with file:line evidence.
- A numbered REQUIRED-CHANGES list (concrete: "add column X, move Y behind Z", not "consider").
- Explicitly: where I am WRONG in my nine findings above.
- Explicitly: what in this plan is over-engineering or premature.
Be blunt. Length is fine. Do not write files; read-only.
