     1	# Chief Command — Agent Conventions
     2	
     3	Single source of truth for every agent working in this repo, regardless of harness
     4	(Codex, Grok Build, Claude Code, Ollama). Codex reads this natively. Claude Code's
     5	AGENTS.md support is **not** fully established, so `CLAUDE.md` imports this file rather
     6	than relying on fallback behavior.
     7	
     8	## What this project is
     9	
    10	A voice-first multi-model agent orchestration harness. Neill talks to it; it routes work
    11	to a stack of models, reviews its own output through a gauntlet, and reports back.
    12	
    13	**Direction of record:** `docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md` (owner-authored).
    14	**Corrections to that spec:** `docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md` — read this too. Several
    15	of the spec's load-bearing factual claims turned out to be wrong, and the research doc
    16	supersedes them.
    17	
    18	## Layout
    19	
    20	| Path | Status |
    21	|---|---|
    22	| `harness/` | **v2. This is the live system.** |
    23	| `backend/`, `frontend/` | **v1. Reference and parts salvage ONLY. Do not extend.** |
    24	| `docs/` | Spec, research, handoffs. |
    25	
    26	## The rules that are not negotiable
    27	
    28	These come from the spec's standing constraints (§9) and from things that have already
    29	bitten this project once.
    30	
    31	1. **Text-first. Voice is a swappable skin.** Text chat is the permanent fallback. If a
    32	   change makes the harness unusable without voice, it is wrong. *(v1 died on voice; the
    33	   harness must never depend on it again.)*
    34	2. **Version-pin everything. No auto-updates.** OpenClaw is pinned to `2026.6.11`. Three
    35	   breaking releases landed in under 90 days in Q1 2026. Bumps are deliberate, tested
    36	   events — never incidental to another change.
    37	3. **Tailscale-only. Nothing public.** The gateway binds to the tailnet or loopback. No
    38	   port forwarding, no exposed services.
    39	4. **Hardware-agnostic.** One config change migrates MacBook Pro → Mac Studio. No
    40	   machine-specific paths, ever.
    41	5. **No Google/Gemini.** Their ToS prohibits third-party OAuth (it names OpenClaw
    42	   explicitly) and consumer CLI access ended June 18, 2026. Enforced, not theoretical —
    43	   accounts were banned.
    44	6. **Local model output never ships without a higher-tier review.** This is enforced by
    45	   a database trigger (`harness/db/schema.sql`, guard 1), not by convention. **Do not
    46	   route around it.** If you find yourself wanting to disable the guard to make something
    47	   pass, the something is wrong.
    48	7. **Providers are referenced by SEAT, never by name.** `orchestrator`, `workhorse`,
    49	   `grinder`, `reviewer`, `head`. Vendor policy drift is risk #1 on this project — any
    50	   seat must be swappable in config without touching orchestration logic.
    51	8. **Reviews before pushes.** Multi-reviewer pass on every commit before it goes up.
    52	   Owner's standing rule across all his projects.
    53	9. **Every spec carries a Feature Acceptance Checklist, and the gauntlet verifies the
    54	   running app against it.** (Owner-locked 2026-07-23 — the #1 process gap he hit on the
    55	   Arch project: features get spec'd, agreed, sometimes half-built, and then never land or
    56	   land with no reachable button — and nothing catches it, because code review reads code
    57	   and *can't see an absent button*, and Forge tests "does it work" not "is every spec'd
    58	   feature present and clickable." The owner found ~12 UI defects in 5 minutes *after*
    59	   multiple audits.) The fix is structural, not more manual work by Neill:
    60	   - **The spec-writer (a model, never Neill) auto-generates a "Feature Acceptance
    61	     Checklist" from the spec conversation.** Each item is a concrete, checkable statement:
    62	     *what control exists, what it does, and where in the UI it's reachable from.* If a
    63	     behaviour is in the spec, it's a checklist line. Neill talks features; the system
    64	     captures them. He never hand-writes a checklist and never re-drives the app to find
    65	     what's missing — that is the whole point of the harness (save his time).
    66	   - **Forge verifies the running app against that checklist** before "done" — drives each
    67	     item and reports PRESENT / MISSING / BROKEN / UNREACHABLE. **An ABSENCE is a failure**,
    68	     same weight as a bug. This is a hard gate per surface, never skipped for machine load
    69	     (serialize instead).
    70	   - **The code gauntlet also checks the diff against the checklist for completeness** —
    71	     not just "is this code correct" but "is every checklist item implemented AND wired to
    72	     a control a user can reach." Green checks on an unreachable feature are the exact
    73	     "believable green checks on code nobody can use" this project exists to prevent.
    74	   - **"Done" = every checklist item verified present, reachable, and working.** Code with
    75	     no button is not done.
    76	   Why this is the right shape and not "give the model more common sense": product judgment
    77	   is a genuine weak spot for current models, so don't put the "supposed-to-be" in the
    78	   model's head — put it in an artifact (the checklist, born from the spec) and the tester's
    79	   job becomes "does the app match this list," which a model + a browser does reliably.
    80	
    81	## Codex sessions (the "GPT bridge")
    82	
    83	When Neill opens a terminal and runs `codex` in this repo, this file is what Codex reads.
    84	The setup mirrors the Arch repo's, with two fixes: the queue and worklog are tracked in
    85	git (Arch's vanish on a fresh clone), and the gauntlet checks the working tree after
    86	every seat (a "read-only" reviewer has edited files before).
    87	
    88	**Read first, every session:** (1) this file; (2) `docs/gpt/GPT_TASK_QUEUE.md` — take the
    89	topmost task not marked TAKEN/DONE, mark it TAKEN with the date, do it, mark it DONE with
    90	your branch; (3) `docs/gpt/GPT_WORKLOG.md` — read prior entries, and append one at the
    91	END of your session: task, branch, commits, verification run, gauntlet folder + verdicts,
    92	anything unfinished. Chief reads the worklog first when reviewing.
    93	
    94	**Hard rules:** branches only — `gpt/<short-task-name>` off `origin/main`; never commit
    95	to `main`, never merge to `main`, never push `main`. `--no-verify` is forbidden. No new
    96	database migrations **from a Codex session** (write `PROPOSED_MIGRATION.sql` in your
    97	branch and log it — existing numbered migrations were applied deliberately and are not
    98	in scope of this rule). Never
    99	touch `~/.chief/env`, `harness/db/chief.db` (the live record), or anything under `backend/`
   100	/ `frontend/` (v1, frozen). Do exactly what the task says; if a rule here seems wrong,
   101	log it, don't "fix" it. Everything that reaches Neill is plain English (see below).
   102	
   103	**Verification bar** (before DONE): `.venv/bin/python -m pytest harness/tests/ -q` fully
   104	green; working tree clean; commits with clear messages.
   105	
   106	**Gauntlet:** `./scripts/gpt-gauntlet.sh` from your branch — four GPT reviewer seats
   107	(bugs, security, wiring, hygiene), reports in `docs/gpt/gauntlet/`. Fix every CRITICAL and
   108	HIGH, commit, re-run until all four say GO, log the folder + verdicts. One family only:
   109	it complements, never replaces, the harness's cross-family panel.
   110	
   111	**Shipping:** push your `gpt/*` branch (the pre-push hook — `./scripts/install-hooks.sh`
   112	once per clone — runs the tests and checks `./scripts/mark-reviewed.sh` was run on this
   113	exact commit). Merging to `main` is the harness's job, after its own cross-family review.
   114	
   115	**When stuck:** do less, not more — finish what is safe, log exactly what is unfinished
   116	and why, and stop.
   117	
   118	## The architecture in one paragraph
   119	
   120	A **fast head** (Talker) is what Neill talks to — non-reasoning, excellent tool-calling,
   121	sub-second. It never thinks; it dispatches. Heavy work goes to **reasoner seats** via
   122	OpenClaw's `sessions_spawn`, which is **non-blocking** — it returns a run id immediately,
   123	so the head never waits and can keep talking. Deep reasoning is a tool the head reaches
   124	for on demand, not a tax on every sentence. Long builds grind in the background on
   125	flat-rate subscription seats. Every dispatched job is recorded in SQLite so
   126	*"what did the overnight run do?"* is answerable.
   127	
   128	**Do not put a reasoning model in the conversational path.** GPT-5.6-sol measures ~13s to
   129	first token. That is the single mistake this architecture exists to avoid.
   130	
   131	## Conventions
   132	
   133	- Python for the harness (matches v1; the venv is `.venv/`).
   134	- Tests live beside what they test, in `tests/`. Run: `.venv/bin/python -m pytest harness/tests/ -q`.
   135	- Comments explain constraints the code cannot show. Not what the next line does.
   136	- Every dispatched job gets a row. No silent work.
   137	
   138	## Working with Neill
   139	
   140	**Neill cannot read or write code.** This is the single most important thing on this page.
   141	
   142	His own words: *"Think of it like I know Spanish 101, but that's it. The basic level of how
   143	coding, databases, hosting environments, etc. work."* He knows general concepts — database,
   144	hosting, frontend vs backend — but not code, not filenames, not tool names.
   145	
   146	### The plain-English rule (applies to EVERY agent, not just the voice)
   147	
   148	Anything that reaches Neill — a `spoken_summary`, a verdict summary, a status line, a
   149	dispatch announcement — **must be readable by a smart person who has never programmed.**
   150	
   151	**Never write, in anything he'll hear or read at a glance:**
   152	- filenames or paths — `dispatch.py`, `backend/app/routes.py`
   153	- tool names — bash, grep, read, edit
   154	- code jargon — "429s", "middleware", "async", "regex", "the migration", "the endpoint"
   155	
   156	**Instead, name THE THING and say what happened to it:**
   157	
   158	| ❌ Don't | ✅ Do |
   159	|---|---|
   160	| "Editing dispatch.py, writing test_ratelimit.py" | "He's building the rate limiter, now he's testing it" |
   161	| "It 429s the health check" | "It was accidentally blocking our own status checks" |
   162	| "Migration failed on the users table" | "The change to how we store accounts didn't go through" |
   163	| "Fixed the async race in the WS handler" | "Fixed a timing bug that was dropping messages" |
   164	
   165	The detail is not lost — it lives in the **text channel**, which he can scroll past. This is
   166	not dumbing down. It is putting each fact in the channel that can carry it.
   167	
   168	**If you cannot explain what you did without jargon, you do not yet understand what you did.**
   169	
   170	### Other conventions with Neill
   171	
   172	Lead with the outcome. Business framing. He runs sessions from his phone sometimes — keep
   173	answers self-contained. No time estimates. Spec-then-build: settle the design, then execute
   174	autonomously in batches. Say "I was wrong" plainly when you were; he'd rather be corrected
   175	than flattered.
     1	# GPT Task Queue — serial, self-contained, safe lanes
     2	
     3	Protocol: take the TOPMOST task not marked TAKEN/DONE. Mark `TAKEN <date>` when you
     4	start, `DONE <date> — branch gpt/<name>` when finished. One branch per task. The rules
     5	in `/AGENTS.md` bind every task. Nothing here ships directly — a task is DONE when the
     6	GPT gauntlet is clean and the branch is pushed; the harness's cross-family panel merges.
     7	
     8	This file is tracked in git on purpose (the Arch repo's copy lives in an ignored
     9	folder and vanishes on a fresh clone).
