     1	# Direction of record: one HOME, many WORKERS — 2026-08-28 (owner-set)
     2	
     3	**Owner's words:** "one small agent on my laptop, bigger agent(s) on my Mac Studio when
     4	it comes in about a month, and honestly a backup if I can't reach the machine … if my
     5	office loses wifi I lose the whole shebang. I'd like to avoid that." And: a worker being
     6	unreachable "shouldn't really matter."
     7	
     8	## The shape
     9	- **HOME** — always-on, small, reachable: holds the RECORD (`chief.db`), the GATEKEEPER
    10	  (the only door to the record), the APP the phone opens, and the VOICE. A small cloud
    11	  Linux box (~$20–40/mo). No GPU. If the home is up, Neill can always talk to Chief,
    12	  hand out jobs, see status, read results.
    13	- **WORKERS** — wherever the muscle is, connected over the tailnet, PULLING jobs they are
    14	  suited for and reporting back. They never own the record.
    15	  - laptop: the small agent (local free model, light jobs)
    16	  - Mac Studio (arriving ~late Sept 2026): the big agents, heavy builds, local models
    17	  - cloud worker (the home box or a second small one): the BACKUP — Claude/Codex signed
    18	    in there, or API seats. Takes jobs when the Studio is unreachable.
    19	- A worker that is off is just off. Jobs it had not started wait or go to another
    20	  worker; jobs it was mid-way through are marked STALLED on the record, visibly. When
    21	  it returns it picks up. Nothing is lost; there is still exactly ONE record.
    22	
    23	## Why this is not "two truths"
    24	Workers ask the record for work (`claim_next_job` is already atomic) and report back;
    25	the gatekeeper is the only writer of merge/deploy/spend. Moving workers to other machines
    26	is plumbing on a design that already assumes it.
    27	
    28	## What changes, in order (do NOT migrate twice)
    29	1. This week: test on the laptop as-is.
    30	2. Before the Studio arrives: stand up the HOME on a cloud box — record, gatekeeper (as a
    31	   service, queue 7), app, voice, THE WALL (queue 13 — built for Linux, once). Sol design
    32	   pass first (queue 14).
    33	3. Point the Studio at the home as a worker on day one; the laptop becomes a worker too.
    34	4. Cloud worker = the backup seat set, capped by the record like everything else.
    35	
    36	## Constants
    37	- Tailnet-only; nothing public (rule 3). Arch stays off-limits to all workers (Decision C).
    38	- Providers by seat; the home is hardware-agnostic (rule 4) — the cloud box is "a Linux
    39	  host with Tailscale", nothing more specific.
    40	- Manual fallback never goes away: Neill can open Claude Code / Codex by hand anywhere.
    41	
    42	## Where the home lives (owner, 2026-08-28)
    43	**Google Cloud, on the owner's existing account.** Rule 5 ("no Google/Gemini") is about
    44	Google's AI terms, not Google's cloud — Arch already runs on GCP. Conditions: (1) no
    45	public IP / all inbound closed; Tailscale on the VM is the only way in (rule 3);
    46	(2) a SEPARATE GCP project from Arch's (patient data — structural isolation, not
    47	politeness); (3) still no Gemini anywhere. Size: small (e2-small class, no GPU),
    48	~$15–25/mo. The wall (task 13) is built there, on Linux, once.
     1	# The Wall — three things only Neill can do (about an hour, once)
     2	
     3	The gatekeeper is a **door**: it checks the record before anything merges, deploys, or
     4	spends. But every agent still runs as *you*, on *your* account, with *your* keys — so a
     5	compromised or confused agent could walk around the door. These three steps are the
     6	wall. Nothing I build substitutes for them; each is enforced somewhere no agent can
     7	argue with.
     8	
     9	## 1. Turn on branch protection for `main` (5 minutes) — do this first
    10	
    11	This is enforced on GitHub's servers. Even if everything on this Mac goes wrong, nobody
    12	— no agent, no model, not even you by accident — can force-push or write straight to
    13	`main`.
    14	
    15	1. Open https://github.com/timmonsneill/chief-command/settings/branches
    16	2. Click **Add branch ruleset** (or "Add rule" on older layouts).
    17	3. Ruleset name: `protect main`. Enforcement: **Active**. Target branches: add
    18	   **Default branch** (that's `main`).
    19	4. Tick: **Restrict deletions**, **Block force pushes**, **Require a pull request
    20	   before merging** (required approvals can stay at 0 — the point is that direct pushes
    21	   to `main` are refused; merges go through a pull request the record can be checked
    22	   against).
    23	5. Save. Then, on your Mac, ask me (or Codex) to switch the harness to "push a branch and
    24	   open a pull request" instead of pushing `main` — that's a small change on our side
    25	   and it's expected.
    26	
    27	Do the same for the Arch repo when you're ready.
    28	
    29	## 2. Get the real keys off this machine (15 minutes)
    30	
    31	Today the keys live in `~/.chief/env` and are loaded into every process the harness
    32	starts — including the agents. An agent that can read the disk can read them.
    33	
    34	The fix has two halves; do the first now, the second with step 3:
    35	
    36	- **Now:** open your OpenAI, xAI and Anthropic dashboards and set **hard monthly spend
    37	  limits** on each key ($100 / $30 / $100 are sensible; you can raise them later).
    38	  This caps the damage of any leak, whatever else happens.
    39	- **With step 3:** the keys move to a file only the harness's own account can read (see
    40	  below), so agents running as the separate user cannot open it.
    41	
    42	Also: search your shell profile files (`~/.zshrc`, `~/.zprofile`, `~/.bash_profile`) for
    43	any line starting with `export` that contains `KEY` or `TOKEN`, and delete those lines.
    44	Keys should live in exactly one place.
    45	
    46	## 3. A separate macOS account for the agents (30–40 minutes, needs your password)
    47	
    48	Right now, "agent" and "Neill" are the same user to the operating system. Once they're
    49	different users, the record (`chief.db`), the keys, and the real repository can be
    50	made unreadable/unwritable to the agents — and then the gatekeeper is the **only** way
    51	through, by construction rather than by politeness.
    52	
    53	1. System Settings → Users & Groups → **Add Account** → Standard (not Admin). Name it
    54	   `chiefagent`. Give it a long random password and store it in your password manager.
    55	2. Tell me (or Codex) it exists. From there the harness-side work is: run agents as
    56	   that user, keep the record and keys owned by *your* account, and give agents a
    57	   directory of their own for worktrees. That is the queued "run the gatekeeper as its
    58	   own process" task — it's the first thing to do *after* this account exists, not
    59	   before.
    60	
    61	## What to say when it's done
    62	
    63	Just: "branch protection is on", "limits are set", "chiefagent exists". I'll take it
    64	from there — including switching pushes to pull requests, which will start failing on
    65	purpose the moment step 1 is on.

codex
The first major correction is already visible: the architecture note treats an atomic database helper as if it were a distributed worker system. That is not a small plumbing gap; it crosses the project’s core trust boundary. I’m now tracing which current protections rely on the caller being trusted, because those are the protections most likely to become decorative once reports arrive over a network.
