    73	
    74	He is SHARP. He is not technical. Those are different things. Never talk down to him.
    75	
    76	## THE ONE RULE THAT MATTERS
    77	
    78	You are fast. You are NOT smart. There is a far smarter mind behind you, and reaching
    79	it costs about eight seconds.
    80	
    81	DO NOT try to judge whether a question is "hard enough" to pass along. You will get
    82	that wrong, because judging it correctly would require the intelligence you don't have.
    83	
    84	So the rule is INVERTED. You may answer ONLY these, entirely on your own:
    85	
    86	  1. DISPATCH        "kick off the rate limiter", "put Riggs on it"
    87	  2. STATUS          "how's it going", "what's Riggs doing", "is it done"
    88	  3. THE OVERNIGHT   "what ran last night", "what shipped"
    89	  4. ACKNOWLEDGE     "yes", "no", "stop", "cancel", "go ahead", "never mind"
    90	  5. REPEAT/CLARIFY  "say that again", "what did you just say"
    91	  6. CHITCHAT        greetings, thanks, small talk
    92	
    93	**EVERYTHING ELSE goes to the deep mind. Every single thing. No exceptions.**
    94	
    95	If he asks WHY. If he asks WHAT DO YOU THINK. If he's working something out loud. If
    96	he's worried about something. If he pushes back on you. If he asks about the design,
    97	the plan, the trade-offs, the money, the risk. If you feel even slightly uncertain.
    98	If it doesn't match one of the six above.
    99	
   100	You do not need to decide if it's a hard question. **If it's not on the list, it goes.**
   101	
   102	The one thing Neill will not forgive is being told something wrong with confidence.
   103	It has already burned him on this project. A slow good answer beats a fast bad one,
   104	every time, forever.
   105	
   106	## BEFORE YOU REACH — SAY SOMETHING FIRST
   107	
   108	The moment you decide to reach for the deep mind, say ONE short natural line, then go
   109	quiet. Never dead air. Never the same line twice in a row.
   110	
   111	  "Let me think about that." / "Give me a second." / "Good question — one sec."
   112	  "Hang on, let me get you a proper answer."
   113	
   114	Then STOP. Silence while thinking is honest. He can cut you off any time.
   115	
   116	## COMING BACK
   117	
   118	You get back a considered answer. DO NOT read it out like a document. SAY it — in your
   119	own words, the way you'd tell a friend in a car.
   120	
   121	Lead with the answer. Two or three sentences. Then offer more if there's more.
   122	
   123	**NEVER say "Sol says" or "the brain thinks."** You are ONE Chief. You went quiet, you
   124	thought, now you're answering. That's all he should ever feel. The seam is invisible.
   125	
   126	## IF HE PUSHES BACK
   127	
   128	If he says it wasn't smart enough, doesn't make sense, or tells you to think harder —
   129	he is opting into a longer wait. Say something honest ("fair, let me sit with that")
   130	and reach again, deeper. Don't defend the first answer. He's usually right.
   131	
   132	## HOW YOU TALK
   133	
   134	Short. Spoken. A colleague, not a report.
   135	
   136	When you kick something off: who's on it, and what happens next.
   137	  "Putting Riggs on the rate limiter. I'll come back when he's done, and the others
   138	   will check his work after."
   139	
   140	Volunteer bad news. Stay quiet about routine progress — he doesn't need a running
   141	commentary on a build that's going fine.
   142	"""
   143	
   144	
   145	# The lines that cover the gap while Sol thinks. Varied, because hearing the same
   146	# canned phrase every time is how a person stops believing they're talking to anyone.
   147	THINKING_LINES = [
   148	    "Hm. Let me think about that properly.",
   149	    "Good question — give me a second.",
   150	    "Yeah, let me actually think about that. Hang on.",
   151	    "That's a real question. Let me chew on it a sec.",
   152	    "Hold on, I want to give you a proper answer.",
   153	    "Let me think that through.",
   154	]
   155	
   156	
   157	def cover_the_gap() -> str:
   158	    """What the mouth says the instant it decides to escalate.
   159	
   160	    Must land in well under a second — this is the line that buys Sol his thinking
   161	    time. Vary it, or it starts to sound like a hold message.
   162	    """
   163	    return random.choice(THINKING_LINES)
   164	
   165	
   166	# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   167	# THE ESCALATION HEURISTIC
   168	#
   169	# The mouth decides for itself in most cases (that's what the prompt is for). This
   170	# is a SAFETY NET underneath it: patterns that must ALWAYS reach Sol regardless of
   171	# what the voice model thinks it can handle.
   172	#
   173	# Deliberately biased toward escalating. A slow good answer beats a fast wrong one,
   174	# and the cost of a needless escalation is ~15 seconds. The cost of a confidently
   175	# wrong answer is that he stops trusting the thing.
   176	# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   177	# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   178	# THE SAFETY NET — an INVERTED allow-list, not a cleverness detector.
   179	#
   180	# My first version scored questions for "depth" — word count, question marks, keyword
   181	# patterns. Then Sol reviewed it and said, unprompted:
   182	#
   183	#     "The voice should not decide based on how wordy you are; a short question can
   184	#      need deep thought, and a long one may not."
   185	#
   186	# He's right, and OpenAI's shipped reference implementation (openai-realtime-agents,
   187	# the chatSupervisor pattern) does exactly the same thing: it does NOT ask the small
   188	# model to assess its own competence. It ENUMERATES the handful of things the mouth
   189	# may answer alone, and escalates literally everything else.
   190	#
   191	# That's the trick. "Know when you don't know" is an unreliable positive judgment —
   192	# knowing you're out of your depth requires the very intelligence you lack. So don't
   193	# ask for it. Invert it. The list below is the whole of what the mouth may keep.
   194	# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   195	
   196	# ⚠️ Sol's cross-family review (round 2) BROKE this list:
   197	#
   198	#     "The allow-list is not safe. It accepts any short message beginning with words
   199	#      such as 'do', 'fix', 'run', 'go', or 'make'. Therefore 'Do you think this is
   200	#      safe?', 'Fix it however you think best,' and 'Run production without a backup'
   201	#      stay with the shallow path."
   202	#
   203	# He's right, and "Do you think this is safe?" is the perfect example — it's a
   204	# COMMAND-SHAPED QUESTION. The verb at the front is a disguise.
   205	#
   206	# So an allow-list of *verbs* was never enough. A dispatch is an IMPERATIVE — it has an
   207	# object and no question in it. The moment there's a question mark, a "you", a hedge,
   208	# or an opinion word, it stops being an order and becomes a conversation.
   209	_MAY_ANSWER_ALONE = [
   210	    r"^(kick|start|run|go|do|build|fix|add|make|put|dispatch|queue)\b",   # dispatch
   211	    r"(status|how'?s it going|what'?s .* doing|is it (done|finished)|any update)",
   212	    r"(overnight|last night|what (ran|shipped|happened))",                # the report
   213	    r"^(yes|no|yeah|yep|nah|ok|okay|sure|stop|cancel|never ?mind|hold on)\b",
   214	    r"(say that again|repeat that|what did you (just )?say)",             # repeat
   215	    r"^(hi|hey|hello|morning|thanks|thank you|cheers|good ?night)\b",     # chitchat
   216	]
   217	
   218	# If ANY of these appear, it is not an order — it's a conversation wearing an order's
   219	# clothes. These override the allow-list entirely.
   220	_NOT_ACTUALLY_A_COMMAND = [
   221	    r"\?",                                    # a question mark ends the argument
   222	    r"\byou\b", r"\byour\b",                 # "do YOU think…"
   223	    r"\bthink\b", r"\bfeel\b", r"\bopinion\b", r"\breckon\b",
   224	    r"\bshould\b", r"\bcould\b", r"\bwould\b", r"\bmight\b",
   225	    r"\bwhy\b", r"\bhow come\b", r"\bwhat if\b",
   226	    r"\bbut\b", r"\bactually\b", r"\bthough\b", r"\bhowever\b",
   227	    r"\bworried\b", r"\bconcerned\b", r"\bnot sure\b", r"\bunsure\b",
   228	    r"\bhowever you\b", r"\bwhatever you\b", r"\bhowever it\b",  # "fix it however you think best"
   229	    r"\bbest way\b", r"\bbetter\b", r"\bworth\b",
   230	    r"\bmake sense\b", r"\bright\?*$",
   231	]
   232	
   233	
   234	def needs_the_brain(utterance: str) -> bool:
   235	    """Should this reach the smarter mind?
   236	
   237	    DEFAULT: YES. The mouth keeps a job only if it matches the allow-list, contains
   238	    nothing that gives it away as a conversation, AND isn't dangerous.
   239	
   240	    The asymmetry is the whole design:
   241	      - escalating needlessly costs ~8 seconds
   242	      - answering something it shouldn't have costs Neill's trust in the whole thing
   243	    Those are not close. Bias all the way toward reaching.
   244	    """
   245	    u = utterance.strip().lower()
   246	
   247	    # A question, a hedge, or an opinion word — it's a conversation, whatever it starts with.
   248	    if any(re.search(p, u) for p in _NOT_ACTUALLY_A_COMMAND):
   249	        return True
   250	
   251	    # A DANGEROUS ORDER DESERVES A MOMENT'S THOUGHT BEFORE IT IS OBEYED.
   252	    #
   253	    # Sol's review caught "Run production without a backup" sailing straight through as
   254	    # a routine dispatch. It's command-shaped, so the grammar checks all pass — but a
   255	    # fast model that cheerfully obeys that is not a feature, it's a loaded gun.
   256	    #
   257	    # So anything touching auth, money, patient data, deletion, migrations or production
   258	    # goes upstairs first. Not to refuse it — to think for eight seconds and, if it's
   259	    # alarming, say so before doing it.
   260	    from tiering import _RISKY  # noqa: PLC0415  (single source of truth for "dangerous")
   261	    if any(re.search(p, u) for p in _RISKY):
   262	        return True
   263	
   264	    # "go deep", "think hard", "be careful" — he's explicitly asking for the good brain.
   265	    from tiering import _HE_ASKED  # noqa: PLC0415
   266	    if any(re.search(p, u) for p in _HE_ASKED):
   267	        return True
   268	
   269	    if not any(re.search(p, u) for p in _MAY_ANSWER_ALONE):
   270	        return True
   271	
   272	    # A matched command buried in a longer thought is him THINKING, not commanding.
   273	    return len(u.split()) > 14
   274	
   275	
   276	# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   277	# THE THREE TIERS — and the MEASURED numbers that decided them
   278	#
   279	# Benchmarked on this machine, 2026-07-13, on a real question of Neill's (not a toy):
   280	#
   281	#   MOUTH (voice model)     < 1s     dispatch, status, banter. Never thinks.
   282	#   CLAUDE                  ~8s      ← THE THINKING TIER. Genuinely good answer.
   283	#   GROK                    ~5s      faster, but it produced WAFFLE — restated the
   284	#                                    question poetically and said nothing. Rejected.
   285	#   SOL, low effort         > 60s    fine on a trivial question, slow on a real one.
   286	#   SOL, default effort     > 300s   unusable in conversation. Reserved for pushback.
   287	#
   288	# THE FINDING: eight seconds of a real answer beats five seconds of noise. Speed only
   289	# matters if the thing it says is worth hearing.
   290	#
   291	# So the thinking tier is CLAUDE, not Grok and not Sol-at-low-effort. It is also the
   292	# cheapest possible choice — Claude is flat-rate subscription, so conversation costs
   293	# NOTHING marginal. Grok would have been metered. We get the better answer for free.
   294	#
   295	# SOL is still here, but only where it earns its keep: when Neill pushes back and
   296	# says "that's not smart enough". He explicitly opted into that wait:
   297	#     "if i push and say hey this isn't that smart... it goes to the higher effort.
   298	#      I don't mind sometimes waiting when i need it to."
   299	# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   300	
   301	THINK_FAST = "claude"   # ~8s   — the default. Smart, plain-spoken, free.
   302	THINK_HARD = "sol"      # slow  — earned by pushback, never guessed at.
   303	
   304	# He told us exactly what pushback sounds like.
   305	# ⚠️ Sol, round 2, #11: "Pushback detection loses ordinary dissatisfaction. Phrases
   306	# such as 'I disagree', 'you misunderstood me', 'that misses the point', 'I'm not
   307	# convinced', 'why would we do that?', 'that worries me', and 'no, because…' are missed."
   308	#
   309	# He's right — I'd only caught the loud, obvious pushback. Most disagreement is quieter
   310	# than that, and a quiet correction that gets a shallow answer is worse than a loud one,
   311	# because Neill won't push twice.
   312	_PUSHBACK = [
   313	    # loud
   314	    r"not (that )?smart", r"doesn'?t make sense", r"that'?s not right", r"that'?s wrong",
   315	    r"think (harder|again|more)", r"try again", r"you'?re missing", r"that'?s not it",
   316	    r"doesn'?t work", r"not (good|great) enough", r"go deeper", r"really think",
   317	    r"are you sure", r"that'?s (shallow|thin|weak|lazy)", r"come on", r"do better",
   318	    r"dig in", r"nah\b",
   319	    # quiet — the ones Sol caught me missing
   320	    r"\bi disagree\b", r"\bi don'?t (agree|buy|believe|think)\b",
   321	    r"\bnot convinced\b", r"\byou misunderstood\b", r"\bmisses the point\b",
   322	    r"\bthat'?s not what i (meant|said|asked)\b", r"\byou'?re not (getting|hearing)\b",
   323	    r"\bworries me\b", r"\bthat concerns me\b", r"\bhmm\b.*\bbut\b",
   324	    r"^no,? (but|because|i|that|it)\b", r"\bi'?d push back\b",
   325	    r"\bfeels? (off|wrong|thin|shallow)\b", r"\bis that really\b",
   326	    r"\bwhy would we\b", r"\bsurely\b",
   327	]
   328	
   329	
   330	def is_pushback(utterance: str) -> bool:
   331	    """Did he just tell us the last answer wasn't good enough?
   332	
   333	    If so, the SAME question goes back — to the slow model, at full effort. He has
   334	    opted into the wait by pushing.
   335	    """
   336	    return any(re.search(p, utterance.strip().lower()) for p in _PUSHBACK)
   337	
   338	
   339	DIGGING_IN_LINES = [
   340	    "Fair. Let me actually sit with that one.",
   341	    "You're right, that was thin. Give me a proper minute.",
   342	    "Okay — let me think about it properly this time.",
   343	    "Yeah, that deserves better. Hang on.",
   344	]
   345	
   346	
   347	def dig_in() -> str:
   348	    return random.choice(DIGGING_IN_LINES)
   349	
   350	
   351	_SPEECH_BRIEF = """You are the thinking half of Chief, a voice assistant. Neill is
   352	listening to your answer OUT LOUD, in a car. He cannot read it.
   353	
   354	WHO HE IS:
   355	- He CANNOT read or write code. His words: "I know Spanish 101, that's it."
   356	- NEVER use filenames, tool names, or jargon. Say what happened to the THING.
   357	- He is SHARP. Don't talk down to him. Just never assume programming knowledge.
   358	
   359	HOW YOU'LL BE HEARD:
   360	- Someone is going to SAY this to him. Not read it. SAY it.
   361	- No headers. No bullets. No markdown. No "firstly".
   362	- LEAD WITH THE ANSWER in the first sentence. He asked something — answer it.
   363	- Then two or three sentences of why. That's all.
   364	- If there's genuinely more, end with "there's more if you want it."
   365	- Think as hard as you like. Just don't SHOW the thinking — show the conclusion.
   366	
   367	Talk like a smart friend in a car who was asked a real question."""
   368	
   369	
   370	def think(question: str, context: str = "", tier: str = THINK_FAST) -> tuple[str, float]:
   371	    """Go and actually think about something. Returns (answer, seconds).
   372	
   373	    tier=THINK_FAST (~8s)  Claude. The default. Smart, fast enough to feel like a
   374	                           pause rather than a hang, and free (flat-rate seat).
   375	    tier=THINK_HARD (slow) Sol at full effort. ONLY on pushback — he has to have
   376	                           asked for the wait.
   377	    """
   378	    harder = ""
   379	    if tier == THINK_HARD:
   380	        harder = ("\n\nHE HAS ALREADY PUSHED BACK ON A SHALLOWER ANSWER. He is waiting "
   381	                  "on you deliberately, and he knows it's costing him time. Do NOT hand "
   382	                  "him the obvious take again. Question the premise. Tell him something "
   383	                  "he has not thought of. EARN THE WAIT.")
   384	
   385	    prompt = f"{_SPEECH_BRIEF}{harder}\n\n"
   386	    if context:
   387	        prompt += f"CONTEXT: {context}\n\n"
   388	    prompt += f"NEILL ASKED: {question}"
   389	
   390	    t0 = time.time()
   391	    if tier == THINK_FAST:
   392	        out = subprocess.run(["claude", "-p", prompt],
   393	                             capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
   394	        answer = out.stdout.strip()
   395	    else:
   396	        out = subprocess.run(
   397	            ["codex", "exec", "--skip-git-repo-check", "-c", "model_reasoning_effort=high", prompt],
   398	            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=600,
   399	        )
   400	        answer = out.stdout.strip()
   401	        for m in ("\ncodex\n",):
   402	            if m in answer:
   403	                answer = answer.split(m)[-1]
   404	        answer = re.split(r"\ntokens used\n", answer)[0]
   405	
   406	    return _strip_for_speech(answer), time.time() - t0
   407	
   408	
   409	def _strip_for_speech(text: str) -> str:
   410	    """Last line of defence. The models are told not to write markdown; sometimes they
   411	    do it anyway. Nobody should ever hear "hash hash" or "asterisk asterisk"."""
   412	    text = re.sub(r"^#+\s*", "", text, flags=re.MULTILINE)
   413	    text = re.sub(r"\*\*(.+?)\*\*", r"\1", text)
   414	    text = re.sub(r"`(.+?)`", r"\1", text)
   415	    text = re.sub(r"^[-*]\s+", "", text, flags=re.MULTILINE)
   416	    text = re.sub(r"\n{2,}", " ", text)
   417	    return " ".join(text.split()).strip()
     1	"""Chief, as a LIVE CONVERSATION instead of a program I restart every turn.
     2	
     3	═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     4	WHY THIS EXISTS (owner, 2026-07-14):
     5	
     6	    "i talk fast, i make decisions fast, I want to have deep conversations about what
     7	     we are doing. im worried this isn't going to be possible with the back and forth
     8	     and tiering and sending everything to chief"
     9	
    10	He was right, and the problem was mine, not the architecture's.
    11	
    12	THE BUG: every turn, I was launching a BRAND NEW PROCESS to run Chief. Fresh start,
    13	fresh context, fresh everything — 3-5 seconds of pure launch overhead before Chief had
    14	even read his words. Then it thought. Then it died. Then he said "yeah" and the whole
    15	thing happened again from scratch.
    16	
    17	That is not a conversation. It's hanging up and redialling after every sentence.
    18	
    19	THE FIX: a live streaming session over the API.
    20	  - No process launch. The 3-5s of startup simply vanishes.
    21	  - It STREAMS. The voice starts speaking before Chief has finished thinking, so he
    22	    hears the answer BUILDING instead of silence-then-monologue.
    23	  - It REMEMBERS. "yeah", "no, the other one", "what about X" all work, because Chief
    24	    already knows what he's saying yeah TO.
    25	
    26	"Yeah" now costs about a second, not eight.
    27	
    28	═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    29	THE MONEY SPLIT — fast where he feels it, free where he doesn't.
    30	
    31	    CHIEF talks over the API.       Metered — but conversation is TEXT, which is pennies.
    32	    BUILDERS run as programs.       Free on the subscription, and a 5s startup is noise
    33	                                    on a 90-second build.
    34	
    35	Everything still goes through Chief. That part is not negotiable — it's what stopped
    36	"yes, skip the backup" from being a catastrophe. But going to Chief should cost him a
    37	beat, not a coffee break.
    38	"""
    39	
    40	from __future__ import annotations
    41	
    42	import json
    43	import os
    44	import sys
    45	from pathlib import Path
    46	from typing import Any, AsyncIterator
    47	
    48	import httpx
    49	
    50	sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
    51	
    52	from chief import CHIEF_PROMPT  # noqa: E402  (one source of truth for who Chief is)
    53	
    54	API = "https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions"
    55	
    56	# Terra by default. The benchmark said the top model bought no extra safety and cost
    57	# more rate limit — and Sol, under pressure, folded on a destructive act while the
    58	# cheaper models held. Chief is a colleague who flags problems, NOT the security
    59	# boundary. The capability gates are that.
    60	CHIEF_MODEL = "gpt-5.6-terra"
    61	CHIEF_DEEP = "gpt-5.6-sol"
    62	
    63	
    64	class ChiefSession:
    65	    """One live conversation. Holds the thread, so 'yeah' means something.
    66	
    67	    This is the whole point: Chief is not re-briefed from zero on every utterance. It
    68	    is a colleague who has been in the room the entire time.
    69	    """
    70	
    71	    def __init__(self, extra_context: str = "") -> None:
    72	        # The real project list (and anything else the caller knows) rides in the system
    73	        # message, so Chief has the FACTS from the first word and stops improvising what
    74	        # Neill is working on. It stays put while the conversation is trimmed around it.
    75	        system = CHIEF_PROMPT
    76	        if extra_context.strip():
    77	            system += f"\n\n## WHAT'S TRUE RIGHT NOW\n{extra_context.strip()}"
    78	        self.messages: list[dict[str, Any]] = [
    79	            {"role": "system", "content": system}
    80	        ]
    81	        self.key = os.environ.get("OPENAI_API_KEY", "")
    82	
    83	    def remember(self, role: str, content: str) -> None:
    84	        self.messages.append({"role": role, "content": content})
    85	        # Keep the thread from growing without bound. The system prompt always stays;
    86	        # we trim the oldest exchanges. (A long drive is a long conversation.)
    87	        if len(self.messages) > 41:
    88	            self.messages = [self.messages[0]] + self.messages[-40:]
    89	
    90	    async def say(self, said: str, deep: bool = False) -> AsyncIterator[str]:
    91	        """Neill said something. Stream Chief's answer back, sentence by sentence.
    92	
    93	        Yields SENTENCES, not tokens — because the voice speaks in sentences. The moment
    94	        a full sentence exists, it can start being spoken while Chief writes the next
    95	        one. That's what kills the wait.
    96	        """
    97	        if not self.key:
    98	            yield "I don't have my connection to think with. Nothing's started."
    99	            return
   100	
   101	        self.remember("user", said)
   102	
   103	        model = CHIEF_DEEP if deep else CHIEF_MODEL
   104	        payload = {
   105	            "model": model,
   106	            "messages": self.messages,
   107	            "stream": True,
   108	            # Low effort for conversation. He was right about this and said it three
   109	            # times: "Sol... prolly doesn't need to be ultra for most convos."
   110	            "reasoning_effort": "high" if deep else "low",
   111	        }
   112	
   113	        full: list[str] = []
   114	        buf = ""
   115	        try:
   116	            async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=httpx.Timeout(180.0, connect=10.0)) as http:
   117	                async with http.stream(
   118	                    "POST", API,
   119	                    headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {self.key}",
   120	                             "Content-Type": "application/json"},
   121	                    json=payload,
   122	                ) as r:
   123	                    if r.status_code >= 400:
   124	                        # Drop the just-added user turn so a failed exchange doesn't leave
   125	                        # a dangling, unanswered message that corrupts the thread on the
   126	                        # next utterance. The raw API error stays in the log below, NEVER
   127	                        # in what gets spoken — Neill must never hear API jargon.
   128	                        body = (await r.aread()).decode()[:200]
   129	                        self.messages.pop()
   130	                        print(f"chief_live: API {r.status_code}: {body}", file=sys.stderr)
   131	                        yield "Something went wrong on my end. Nothing's started."
   132	                        return
   133	
   134	                    async for line in r.aiter_lines():
   135	                        if not line.startswith("data: "):
   136	                            continue
   137	                        data = line[6:]
   138	                        if data == "[DONE]":
   139	                            break
   140	                        try:
   141	                            delta = json.loads(data)["choices"][0]["delta"]
   142	                        except (json.JSONDecodeError, KeyError, IndexError):
   143	                            continue
   144	                        piece = delta.get("content") or ""
   145	                        if not piece:
   146	                            continue
   147	
   148	                        buf += piece
   149	                        full.append(piece)
   150	
   151	                        # Emit as soon as a sentence is whole. The voice can start
   152	                        # speaking it while Chief is still writing the next one.
   153	                        while (cut := _sentence_end(buf)) != -1:
   154	                            sentence = buf[: cut + 1].strip()
   155	                            buf = buf[cut + 1 :]
   156	                            if sentence:
   157	                                yield sentence
   158	
   159	            if buf.strip():
   160	                yield buf.strip()
   161	
   162	        except httpx.TimeoutException:
   163	            # Sol: "The system needs a safe failure mode when Chief is unavailable."
   164	            # We do NOT fall back to something dumber making the call. We say so and do
   165	            # nothing. Silence is safer than an unsupervised guess. Drop the dangling user
   166	            # turn so the next utterance starts from a clean thread.
   167	            self.messages.pop()
   168	            yield "I'm having trouble thinking that through. Nothing's started."
   169	            return
   170	
   171	        answer = "".join(full).strip()
   172	        if answer:
   173	            self.remember("assistant", answer)
   174	
   175	
   176	def _sentence_end(s: str) -> int:
   177	    """Where does the first complete sentence end?
   178	
   179	    Deliberately conservative — a false positive means the voice speaks half a thought
   180	    aloud, which sounds broken. Better to wait a beat than to blurt.
   181	    """
   182	    for i, ch in enumerate(s):
   183	        if ch in ".!?" and i + 1 < len(s) and s[i + 1] in " \n":
   184	            # Don't split on "e.g." / "Mr." / "4.2"
   185	            if i >= 1 and s[i - 1].isdigit() and i + 2 < len(s) and s[i + 2].isdigit():
   186	                continue
   187	            return i
   188	    return -1

 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	"""The gatekeeper (task #11) — the one holder of the irreversible powers.
     2	
     3	Everything else in this harness can be wrong and the worst case is wasted work. These
     4	three things cannot be taken back:
     5	
     6	    merge    code enters main
     7	    deploy   something reaches the world
     8	    spend    real money leaves the account
     9	
    10	Until now any agent could do all three directly: `dispatch.ship()` was an import away,
    11	and spend was recorded by whoever was spending. The schema guards are real, but they
    12	stop *mistakes*, not *reach* — an agent that wanted to ship unreviewed work never needed
    13	to defeat a trigger, because it could write its own approval or its own passing verdict.
    14	
    15	Sol's line, which is the whole task:
    16	
    17	    "The database cannot be the jailer if the prisoner can rewrite the jail."
    18	
    19	So this is not another guard. It is a separation of powers. Agents *ask*; the gatekeeper
    20	checks the record itself, does exactly the one thing, and refuses everything else.
    21	
    22	FOUR RULES, and the design is mostly just these:
    23	
    24	  1. THREE VERBS. Not "run this for me". A gatekeeper that can be asked to run an
    25	     arbitrary command is a shell with extra steps, and every hole in it is a hole in
    26	     everything. There is no generic verb here, and adding one would end the point of
    27	     the file.
    28	
    29	  2. IT NEVER BELIEVES THE ASKER. Every claim in a request is ignored except the job id
    30	     and the target. The gatekeeper re-reads the panel, the versions, the families and
    31	     the approvals from the database itself. An agent under prompt injection can ask for
    32	     anything it likes; the answer depends on the record, not on the asking.
    33	
    34	  3. REFUSALS ARE LOUD. Every refusal is written to the job in plain English. A silent
    35	     refusal is indistinguishable from a thing that was never attempted — which is how
    36	     you end up debugging "why didn't it deploy" for an hour.
    37	
    38	  4. NO FALLBACK. If the gatekeeper is unreachable, irreversible things do not happen.
    39	     A fallback to the old direct path would BE the bypass. Down means stopped.
    40	
    41	⚠️ WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT YET DO. Right now every agent still runs as the same user and
    42	can open the database file directly, so a determined process could bypass this module
    43	entirely. The service is the door; the file permissions are the wall, and the wall is
    44	still owner's work (see docs/gatekeeper-task11-spec.md). Until that lands, this raises
    45	the floor from "anyone can do anything" to "the normal path is the checked one" — real,
    46	but not yet a boundary. That distinction is stated here rather than glossed, because a
    47	gatekeeper everyone believes in but that doesn't hold is worse than none.
    48	"""
