     1	// Chief Command v2 — seat definitions (spec §4)
     2	//
     3	// Providers sit behind NAMED ROLES so any of them can be swapped without touching
     4	// orchestration logic. Nothing else in the harness may reference a provider by name —
     5	// only by seat id. Vendor policy drift is risk #1 on this project (§9).
     6	//
     7	// Hardware (Phase 0 RAM check, §2): M2 Pro, 16 GB. BELOW the spec's 36 GB threshold,
     8	// so the free grinder is a 7B coder, not the hoped-for 30B.
     9	//
    10	// ===========================================================================
    11	// THE ORGANIZING PRINCIPLE
    12	//
    13	// Every seat answers ONE of two questions: "how fast does it talk?" or "how well
    14	// does it think?" Nothing is good at both, because thinking well MEANS taking time.
    15	// So we don't fight the tension — we seat around it:
    16	//
    17	//   MOUTH   fast, dumb, never blocks. Dispatches and narrates.
    18	//   BRAIN   slow, deep. A dispatch target you are never waiting on.
    19	//   WORKHORSE  the best autonomous coder we can buy. Volume + quality.
    20	//   GRINDERS   cheap parallel attempts. Output NEVER ships unreviewed (DB-enforced).
    21	//   REVIEWER   escalation, highest judgment. Optional and droppable.
    22	// ===========================================================================
    23	
    24	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    25	// MOUTH — the Talker. What Neill actually talks to.
    26	//
    27	// Speech-to-speech: audio in, audio out, ONE model, native tool calling. This is
    28	// what deletes the Whisper + Kokoro + Pipecat-plumbing stack that killed v1.
    29	//
    30	// DECISION DEFERRED ON PURPOSE. Both candidates are GA, sub-second, and speak the
    31	// SAME wire protocol (xAI is OpenAI-Realtime-compatible — you change the base URL).
    32	// So: build against the OpenAI Realtime event schema, keep base_url in config, and
    33	// the other becomes a ONE-LINE swap. Do not marry either one.
    34	//
    35	// DECIDED 2026-08-27 (owner), on current measured numbers, not a blog post:
    36	//   grok-voice-think-fast-2.0  $0.08/min ($4.80/hr)  0.70s to first sound  S2S 82.9
    37	//   grok-voice-think-fast-1.0  $0.05/min ($3.00/hr)  1.25s                 S2S 75.7
    38	//   gpt-realtime-2.1 (high)    ~$10.75/hr measured   1.21s                 S2S 79.1
    39	// Grok 2.0 is faster, better and less than half OpenAI's cost. Google is out (rule 5).
    40	// The voice FEELING dumb is the design (the mouth never answers; everything waits on
    41	// the 13s Chief) — the fix is the fast lane (#12), not a vendor swap. OpenAI stays a
    42	// one-line fallback (same wire protocol). Pinned by exact id; never a -latest alias.
    43	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    44	[seats.mouth]
    45	// ⚠️ WHAT ACTUALLY RUNS (found 2026-08-27): the voice page and its token endpoint in
    46	// harness/server.py + harness/web/voice.html are hardwired to OpenAI's realtime API
    47	// over WebRTC. For weeks this block said "grok" while the code ignored it — a config
    48	// that lies. It now says what runs. The DECIDED target is Grok voice 2.0 (above);
    49	// getting there is a BUILD (xAI's realtime is WebSocket-only, so the browser audio
    50	// path must be rewritten, plus its own token step) — queued as the top voice task.
    51	provider = "openai-realtime"
    52	model = "gpt-realtime-2.1"                  // the FULL model: mini won't reliably call the tool
    53	base_url = "https://api.openai.com/v1/realtime"
    54	family = "gpt"
    55	tier = "metered"
    56	turn_detection = "semantic_vad"             // NOT push-to-talk. Owner override.
    57	eagerness = "low"                           // a driver pauses mid-sentence to merge
    58	interrupt_response = true                   // barge-in
    59	
    60	// VOICE HAS ITS OWN BUDGET, separate from the builders. OpenAI realtime measures
    61	// ~$10.75/hr with reasoning up (~$0.06-0.11/min with caching). 480c/day ≈ 45 min-1 hr.
    62	// The Grok 2.0 target is $4.80/hr for the same hour.
    63	daily_cap_cents = 480
    64	notes = "Fast + dumb BY DESIGN. Never blocks. Target: Grok voice 2.0 (build queued)."
    65	
    66	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    67	// BRAIN — deep reasoning, planning, decomposition, judging the gauntlet.
    68	// SLOW BY DESIGN and that is fine: it is a dispatch target, never a conversation
    69	// partner. Its ~13s time-to-first-token is invisible because you are never waiting.
    70	//
    71	// Ultra mode is literally an orchestrator: it decomposes the task and spawns
    72	// parallel subagents trained to coordinate MID-task rather than merge at the end.
    73	// That is the reviewer-gauntlet shape (§6), native.
    74	//   Terminal-Bench 2.1: 88.8% single-agent / 91.9% Ultra — SOTA for agentic work.
    75	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    76	// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    77	// MODEL TIERS — nothing gets the top model by default. Owner (2026-07-14):
    78	//     "If we do highest model every time for every build, on autonomous work, Claude
    79	//      and ChatGPT are gonna bottom out."
    80	//
    81	// THE KEY FACT: the binding constraint on autonomous work is RATE LIMITS, not money.
    82	// Claude and OpenAI are both flat-rate — you already paid. But you CANNOT BUY YOUR WAY
    83	// OUT OF A WEEKLY CAP. Every top-tier call spent on boilerplate is one you don't have
    84	// left for the thing that actually needed it.
    85	//
    86	// So the top tier is EARNED: you ask for it, the work is dangerous (auth/money/data),
    87	// it already failed twice, a reviewer flagged p0/p1, or it's a decision that's
    88	// expensive to reverse. Everything else runs light or standard. See tiering.py — the
    89	// escalation is a RULE, not a vibe, and every job records which tier it got AND WHY.
    90	// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    91	
    92	[seats.brain]
    93	provider = "codex"
    94	model = "gpt-5.6-sol"
    95	tier = "subscription"
    96	family = "gpt"
    97	
    98	// Owner: "Sol needs to be the brain, but prolly doesn't need to be ultra for most
    99	// convos. Maybe if it's managing a major spec and deciding builds and such, but
   100	// generally lower effort in convo."
   101	//
   102	// Measured, and he's right: Sol at FULL effort took over FIVE MINUTES on a real
   103	// question. In a conversation that isn't thorough — it's a hang. Claude at ~8s gave a
   104	// genuinely better answer. So Sol's heavy tier is reserved for what it's actually for:
   105	// specs, architecture, and pushback.
   106	model_light    = "gpt-5.6-luna"             // quick takes
   107	model_standard = "gpt-5.6-terra"            // most work
   108	model_heavy    = "gpt-5.6-sol"              // specs, decisions, pushback. EARNED.
   109	effort_light    = "low"
   110	effort_standard = "medium"
   111	effort_heavy    = "high"
   112	
   113	mode = "ultra"                              // ONLY at heavy. Coordinated subagents.
   114	max_threads = 6                             // spec said 8; the real default is 6
   115	max_depth = 1
   116	notes = "OAuth is TOLERATED, not sanctioned. Most likely seat to break. Keep API-key swap ready."
   117	
   118	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   119	// WORKHORSE — volume autonomous coding. Migrations, refactors, test backfills.
   120	//
   121	// OWNER DECISION (2026-07-13): OpenAI, not Claude. Rationale below — and note the
   122	// reason is NOT the one in the spec.
   123	//
   124	// ⚠️ THE SPEC'S REASON IS WRONG. It says Claude headless draws from a metered credit
   125	// pool. It does NOT — Anthropic announced that, then PAUSED it before it took effect.
   126	// `claude -p` runs on normal subscription limits TODAY. Claude-as-workhorse would not
   127	// cost extra dollars.
   128	//
   129	// ✅ THE REAL REASON, which nobody had written down: RATE LIMITS, not cost. Running a
   130	// workhorse hard (unattended overnight builds, migrations, backfills) burns Max plan
   131	// usage caps — and it's the SAME bucket as interactive use. The failure mode is nasty:
   132	// your autonomous builder silently eats the model you were going to review its work
   133	// with. So Claude stays OUT of the high-volume seat, and stays IN as low-volume
   134	// escalation (see [seats.reviewer]).
   135	//
   136	// GPT-5.6-sol is not a consolation prize here: 88.8% Terminal-Bench 2.1 (91.9% Ultra),
   137	// SOTA for agentic coding. Flat subscription, predictable, bounded.
   138	//
   139	// For the record, since it may matter on the Studio later:
   140	//     Claude Opus  87.6% SWE-bench Verified   (best autonomous coder available)

