     1	# Direction of record: one HOME, many WORKERS — 2026-08-28 (owner-set)
     2	
     3	**Owner's words:** "one small agent on my laptop, bigger agent(s) on my Mac Studio when
     4	it comes in about a month, and honestly a backup if I can't reach the machine … if my
     5	office loses wifi I lose the whole shebang. I'd like to avoid that." And: a worker being
     6	unreachable "shouldn't really matter."
     7	
     8	## The shape
     9	- **HOME** — always-on, small, reachable: holds the RECORD (`chief.db`), the GATEKEEPER
    10	  (the only door to the record), the APP the phone opens, and the VOICE. A small cloud
    11	  Linux box (~$20–40/mo). No GPU. If the home is up, Neill can always talk to Chief,
    12	  hand out jobs, see status, read results.
    13	- **WORKERS** — wherever the muscle is, connected over the tailnet, PULLING jobs they are
    14	  suited for and reporting back. They never own the record.
    15	  - laptop: the small agent (local free model, light jobs)
    16	  - Mac Studio (arriving ~late Sept 2026): the big agents, heavy builds, local models
    17	  - cloud worker (the home box or a second small one): the BACKUP — Claude/Codex signed
    18	    in there, or API seats. Takes jobs when the Studio is unreachable.
    19	- A worker that is off is just off. Jobs it had not started wait or go to another
    20	  worker; jobs it was mid-way through are marked STALLED on the record, visibly. When
    21	  it returns it picks up. Nothing is lost; there is still exactly ONE record.
    22	
    23	## Why this is not "two truths"
    24	Workers ask the record for work (`claim_next_job` is already atomic) and report back;
    25	the gatekeeper is the only writer of merge/deploy/spend. Moving workers to other machines
    26	is plumbing on a design that already assumes it.
    27	
    28	## What changes, in order (do NOT migrate twice)
    29	1. This week: test on the laptop as-is.
    30	2. Before the Studio arrives: stand up the HOME on a cloud box — record, gatekeeper (as a
    31	   service, queue 7), app, voice, THE WALL (queue 13 — built for Linux, once). Sol design
    32	   pass first (queue 14).
    33	3. Point the Studio at the home as a worker on day one; the laptop becomes a worker too.
    34	4. Cloud worker = the backup seat set, capped by the record like everything else.
    35	
    36	## Constants
    37	- Tailnet-only; nothing public (rule 3). Arch stays off-limits to all workers (Decision C).
    38	- Providers by seat; the home is hardware-agnostic (rule 4) — the cloud box is "a Linux
    39	  host with Tailscale", nothing more specific.
    40	- Manual fallback never goes away: Neill can open Claude Code / Codex by hand anywhere.
    41	
    42	## Where the home lives (owner, 2026-08-28)
    43	**Google Cloud, on the owner's existing account.** Rule 5 ("no Google/Gemini") is about
    44	Google's AI terms, not Google's cloud — Arch already runs on GCP. Conditions: (1) no
    45	public IP / all inbound closed; Tailscale on the VM is the only way in (rule 3);
    46	(2) a SEPARATE GCP project from Arch's (patient data — structural isolation, not
    47	politeness); (3) still no Gemini anywhere. Size: small (e2-small class, no GPU),
    48	~$15–25/mo. The wall (task 13) is built there, on Linux, once.
   140	A screenshot captured for version A would satisfy a tester verdict on version B. Add a
   141	`reviewed_version` (or `head_version` snapshot) column on artifacts, stamped at capture,
   142	and make the guard require `a.reviewed_version IS NEW.reviewed_version`. Numbered
   143	migration + schema.sql in step; `test_live_db_matches_schema` must pass after applying.
   144	Design with Sol first (it's a guard change).
   145	
   146	## 12. [ ] Merge-time bundle contract, a commit-time size limit, and pushing job/N
   147	`gatekeeper.merge` recomputes `git merge-base main tip` at merge time and compares
   148	that FRESH computation against the stored bundle — which means the base it
   149	verifies against can differ from the base the panel actually reviewed against if
   150	`main` moved in between (task #9's design review flagged this: the check can be
   151	correct today and still verify against the wrong thing tomorrow). Store the FULL
   152	base commit id (not just the diff text) and the exact `git diff` options used
   153	(`--no-renames`, pinned) at build time, and make `gatekeeper.merge` verify against
   154	THAT STORED BASE, never a recomputed one — if `main` has moved, that's a new
   155	integration and needs a fresh review, not a silent re-verify. Separately:
   156	`_claude_cli_build`/`_commit_diff_in_clone` have no upper bound on how large a
   157	change they'll accept before committing — a commit-time size limit (mirroring
   158	gauntlet.py's MAX_CODE_CHARS truncation-avoidance design for reviews) belongs here
   159	too, refused BEFORE a commit exists rather than truncated after. And today nothing
   160	ever pushes `job/<id>` anywhere — when auto-merge is eventually enabled (after the
   161	real OS isolation boundary Sol's design gate is still waiting on), the
   162	gatekeeper's merge needs to push the merged `main` to the real remote, behind the
   163	same lock, with the same "refuse and say so" discipline as every other step in
   164	that function. Design each of these with Sol before building, same as task #9
   165	itself was.
   166	
   167	## 13. [ ] Run builders and reviewers as the `chiefagent` account (the wall, harness side)
   168	BLOCKED until the owner creates a standard (non-admin) macOS user `chiefagent` and signs
   169	in to Claude Code + Codex as that user once. Then, in this order, each with a live check:
   170	1. Keys: `~/.chief/env` stays owned by the owner, mode 600. Builders/reviewers receive NO
   171	   vendor keys. The xai reviewer runs in the harness process (owner), not as the agent.
   172	2. Layout: `/Users/chiefagent/work/job-<id>/` owned by chiefagent for clones; the record
   173	   (`harness/db/chief.db`), the real repos and `~/.chief` unreadable to chiefagent
   174	   (`chmod 700` on the owner's home is the simplest — verify Jess/Chief repos too).
   175	3. Launch-as-agent: ONE sudoers rule scoped to exactly one wrapper script
   176	   (`scripts/run-as-agent.sh <job-dir> <argv…>`), no password, nothing else. The wrapper
   177	   sets a minimal env (PATH, HOME=/Users/chiefagent, USER=chiefagent) and cds into the
   178	   job dir. Never `sudo -u chiefagent bash`.
   179	4. Executor: clone into the agent's work dir as the OWNER, `chown -R chiefagent`, run the
   180	   builder through the wrapper; read the result back as the owner. Reviewers (claude-cli,
   181	   codex) likewise through the wrapper — they read untrusted text too.
   182	5. Give builders back `Bash` + `--max-turns`, and run the project's tests inside the
   183	   clone through the wrapper, recording exit_code/stdout artifacts (still NOT a tester
   184	   verdict — guard 6 stays; a real driving tester is separate).
